# Implementation Plan: Port 03_prompt_builder from Ruby to Python

## Goal Description
Port `week1_baseline/ruby/03_prompt_builder` to Python under `week1_baseline/python/03_prompt_builder`. The prompt builder serializes conversation context (system prompt, message history with roles, and registered tools) into the exact JSON API payload format expected by different LLM provider APIs (Anthropic, Gemini, OpenAI, Ollama, and Ollama Cloud) without requiring heavyweight external SDKs.

The port will be fully executable via `week1_baseline/bin/python/03_prompt_builder.sh` within an isolated `.venv`, ensuring 100% output parity with `week1_baseline/bin/ruby/03_prompt_builder.sh`.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **No Global Dependencies**: Like preceding steps (`00_config`, `01_struct_skeleton`, and `02_the_registry`), all Python packages are restricted to a local `.venv` (`week1_baseline/python/03_prompt_builder/.venv`). Global python packages will never be touched or modified.

> [!NOTE]
> **Registry & Context Harmony**:
> In Step 2, `Registry` was decoupled so that it owns tool registration and dispatch. In Step 3, `PromptBuilder` needs tool definitions to serialize schemas for the LLM. 
> To support both decoupled usage (`registry = Registry()`) and backwards compatibility with the Ruby course baseline (`registry = Registry(ctx)` and `PromptBuilder(ctx, backend)`), our design supports:
> 1. `Registry(context=None)`: Stores `self.tools`. If `context` is provided, syncs with `context.tools`.
> 2. `PromptBuilder(context, backend, registry=None)`: Resolves tools from `registry.tools` if passed, or falls back to `getattr(context, "tools", {})`.
> This guarantees clean architecture while maintaining exact script compatibility with Ruby's `example.rb`.

